\section{Metric-Wave Field Theory}
% ============================================================

We define a scalar metric deviation field $\psi(\bm{x}, t)$ governed by:

\begin{equation}
\partial_t \psi
= D_{\text{cont}} \nabla^2 \psi
- K_{\text{local}} \psi
+ \lambda_3 \psi^2
+ \lambda_4 \psi^3
+ \eta \nabla^2 (\partial_t \psi)
+ I_{\text{in}}(\bm{x}, t).
\end{equation}

Parameters:
\begin{align*}
D_{\text{cont}} &= \SI{2.50}{m^2/s}, &
K_{\text{local}} &= \SI{4.18e4}{s^{-1}}, \\
\lambda_3 &= \SI{1.82e6}{s^{-1}}, &
\lambda_4 &= \SI{6.45e7}{s^{-1}}, \\
\eta &= \SI{2.10e3}{s^{-1}}.
\end{align*}

Digital logic is encoded as coherent pulses at carrier frequency
$f_c = \SI{3.98}{GHz}$.

